#21 positive.js:65:1-73.2 - excessive-comments: function body is dominated by comments (6 of 9 non-blank lines are comments).
#22 positive.js:81:13-24 - deep-nesting: control flow nested more than 4 levels deep (depth 5).
#23 positive.js:91:1-176.2 - long-function: function has more than 80 lines of code (86 lines of code).
#24 positive.js:190:34-200.2 - duplicated-block: block of code duplicates another block (first occurrence at positive.js:178:31, lines 178-188).
#25 positive.js:203:17-35 - ts-missing-optional-chain-expression: prefer using an optional chain expression instead, as it's more concise and easier to read (`prev && prev.style` -> `prev?.style`).
#26 positive.js:204:16-55 - ts-missing-optional-chain-expression: prefer using an optional chain expression instead, as it's more concise and easier to read (`list && list.first && list.first.name()` -> `list?.first?.name()`).
#27 positive.js:205:16-31 - ts-missing-optional-chain-expression: prefer using an optional chain expression instead, as it's more concise and easier to read (`map && map[key]` -> `map?.[key]`).
#28 positive.js:210:3-12 - ts-var-declaration: use `let` or `const` instead of `var`, as block-scoped declarations avoid hoisting surprises (`var count` -> `let count`).
#29 positive.js:211:7-27 - ts-loose-equality: use `===` and `!==` instead of `==` and `!=`, as loose equality converts the operands in surprising ways (`count == rows.length` -> `count === rows.length`).
#30 positive.js:212:5-14 - ts-debugger-statement: remove this `debugger` statement, as it pauses the program whenever developer tools are open.
#31 positive.js:214:17-44 - ts-prefer-array-find: prefer `.find()` over taking the first element of `.filter()`, as it stops at the first match and states the intent (`rows.filter((r) => r.ok)[0]` -> `rows.find((r) => r.ok)`).
#32 positive.js:215:16-51 - ts-prefer-array-some: prefer `.some()` over comparing the length of `.filter()`, as it stops at the first match and returns a boolean (`rows.filter((r) => r.ok).length > 0` -> `rows.some((r) => r.ok)`).
#33 positive.js:216:16-54 - ts-prefer-array-some: prefer `.some()` over comparing the length of `.filter()`, as it stops at the first match and returns a boolean (`rows.filter((r) => r.bad).length === 0` -> `!rows.some((r) => r.bad)`).
#34 positive.js:217:17-42 - ts-prefer-includes: prefer `.includes()` over comparing the result of `.indexOf()`, as it states the intent directly (`words.indexOf("x") !== -1` -> `words.includes("x")`).
#35 positive.js:218:16-37 - ts-prefer-at: prefer `.at()` with a negative index over subtracting from `.length`, as it avoids repeating the receiver (`rows[rows.length - 1]` -> `rows.at(-1)`).
#36 positive.js:219:15-63 - ts-prefer-object-has-own: prefer `Object.hasOwn()` over calling `hasOwnProperty` through the prototype, as it is shorter and easier to read (`Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(user, "id")` -> `Object.hasOwn(user, "id")`).
#37 positive.js:220:17-49 - ts-typeof-undefined: compare with `undefined` directly instead of using `typeof`, as it is shorter and easier to read (`typeof user.name === "undefined"` -> `user.name === undefined`).
#38 positive.js:221:17-45 - ts-typeof-undefined: compare with `undefined` directly instead of using `typeof`, as it is shorter and easier to read (`"undefined" !== typeof count` -> `count !== undefined`).
#39 positive.js:222:25-44 - ts-prefer-string-replace-all: prefer `.replaceAll()` over `.replace()` with a global regex, as it states the intent (`.replace(/-/g, "_")` -> `.replaceAll("-", "_")`).
#40 positive.js:223:16-39 - ts-number-static-methods: prefer the static methods of `Number` over the global functions, as they keep number helpers in one namespace (`parseInt(user.size, 10)` -> `Number.parseInt(user.size, 10)`).
#41 positive.js:224:25-40 - ts-prefer-node-protocol: prefer the `node:` protocol when importing a Node.js builtin module, as it makes clear that the module is a builtin (`"child_process"` -> `"node:child_process"`).
#42 positive.js:225:28-41 - ts-prefer-node-protocol: prefer the `node:` protocol when importing a Node.js builtin module, as it makes clear that the module is a builtin (`"fs/promises"` -> `"node:fs/promises"`).
#43 positive.js:230:8-13 - ts-var-declaration: use `let` or `const` instead of `var`, as block-scoped declarations avoid hoisting surprises (`var i` -> `let i` before the enclosing block, as later code uses it).
#44 positive.js:237:3-8 - ts-var-declaration: use `let` or `const` instead of `var`, as block-scoped declarations avoid hoisting surprises (`var n` -> `let n`).
#45 positive.js:239:5-10 - ts-var-declaration: use `let` or `const` instead of `var`, as block-scoped declarations avoid hoisting surprises (`var n` -> `n`, as the function already declares it).
#46 positive.js:245:14-247.5 - ts-prefer-string-replace-all: prefer `.replaceAll()` over `.replace()` with a global regex, as it states the intent (`.replace(/[<>]/g, ...)` -> `.replaceAll(/[<>]/g, ...)`).
#47 positive.js:250:23-41 - no-formatted-string-placeholders: formatted string without any placeholders (``no substitutions`` -> `"no substitutions"`).
#48 positive.js:252:4-26 - change-narration-comment: comment describes the edit history instead of the code (`The old implementation`).
#49 positive.js:257:3-15 - tautological-assertion: assertion compares literals or asserts a literal true, so it cannot fail (`assert(true)`).
#50 positive.js:258:10-56 - cheerful-message: chatty or celebratory string literal ("Oops", "Yay", "Great!", "successfully!") (`Congrats, the items were saved successfully!`).
